  :prose
"Before there were graphics cards there was a dot on an oscilloscope, and
before there were dots there was an equation. `y = y0 + v*t + 0.5*a*t*t`.
Newton wrote it down; a Cambridge student pointed a beam at a phosphor
screen; a bored technician noticed that if you added a floor and flipped
the sign of velocity on contact, the dot bounced. That is not a myth.
That is 1958, and it was called Tennis for Two, and it played on an
analog computer at a Long Island lab open house.

Every arcade game descends from that dot. Break out its rules and you get
the whole family. Add a paddle, you have Pong. Add many bricks, you have
Breakout. Rotate the coordinate system ninety degrees and add a cannon
shell, you have Space Invaders. But the parent of all of them is one
sprite falling under gravity and rebounding off a floor.

This chapter builds that parent. One entity. One law. One floor. By the
end you will have written a rainstorm that piles into a corner because
you clicked there, and you will understand the bounce coefficient as
'how much speed survives the wall.' That is enough physics to carry you
through the next fifteen chapters.

---

## 1.1 The smallest possible game

```scheme
;; A body is a (pos vel) pair of vecs; the world is a list of bodies.
(define (body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (body-pos b) (car b))
(define (body-vel b) (cadr b))

;; One frame of physics: velocity += gravity, position += velocity,
;; then clamp to the floor and flip+damp the vertical velocity.
(define (step b gravity floor-y bounce)
  (let* ((v (vec/add (body-vel b) gravity))
         (p (vec/add (body-pos b) v)))
    (if (> (cadr p) floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car p) floor-y)
              (vec/make (car v) (* -1 bounce (cadr v))))
        (body p v))))

;; A single dot falls under gravity and settles on the floor.
(define (drop-one-dot)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 40) (vec/make 0 0)))   ;; start high
             (n 60))
    (if (= n 0)
        (cadr (body-pos b))                    ;; final y
        (loop (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.0) (- n 1)))))

(drop-one-dot)
;; => 240
```

That is a game. Not a fun game, but a game. There is a rule (gravity),
a boundary (floor), a subject (dot), and a resolution (dot rests at 240).
Everything else in this book adds detail to those four elements.

A `body` is just a plain value: a `(pos vel)` pair, each of which is a
vec built with `vec/make`. `step` is a pure function — hand it a body
and it returns the next body one frame later. Inside it, `vec/add` on
`velocity + gravity` accelerates the body; `vec/add` on `position +
velocity` moves it. Gravity is `(vec/make 0 0.5)`: zero sideways,
half-a-pixel-per-frame-squared straight down. The floor is a plain
number, `240`; when the body's y crosses it we clamp y back to 240 and
multiply the vertical velocity by `-bounce`. With `bounce` = 0.0 all
vertical speed is absorbed — a thud. The `let loop` idiom is Scheme's
named-let: a helper called `loop` we immediately call, threading the body
through 60 frames.

Every game in this book is a variation on this shape.

---

## 1.2 Let it bounce

Change one number. Change the meaning of the whole scene.

```scheme
;; Same fall, but the dot keeps 85% of its speed on each bounce.
;; Fall to the floor, then climb back and report the rebound peak
;; (the smallest y — highest point — reached after the first bounce).
(define (bouncing-dot)
  (let fall ((b (body (vec/make 160 40) (vec/make 0 0))))
    (if (>= (cadr (body-pos b)) 240)
        (let climb ((b2 (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.85)) (top 240))
          (if (>= (cadr (body-vel b2)) 0)            ;; started falling again
              top
              (climb (step b2 (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.85)
                     (min top (cadr (body-pos b2))))))
        (fall (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.85)))))

(bouncing-dot)
;; => 104.29999999999987
```

At bounce 0.85, the ball rebounds strongly the first time, less strongly
the second, less still the third, and so on. Each impact loses fifteen
percent of its speed. Drop it from y=40 to a floor at 240 and the first
rebound climbs back to about y=104 — roughly the "105 third-peak" figure
below, because the fall converts to speed and 0.85 of it returns. The
bounce heights form a decaying series: 200 first, 145 second, 105 third.
The ratio between successive peaks is the
bounce coefficient squared (0.85 * 0.85 = 0.72), because each bounce
loses (1 - bounce^2) fraction of energy — the ball's kinetic energy
scales with velocity squared.

That is not trivia. If you want a super-ball, set bounce close to 1. If
you want a heavy sandbag, set bounce to 0. If you want realistic rubber,
0.7 to 0.85. If you want a sad thump, 0.2. Appendix A tabulates the
whole spectrum with predicted behavior; if you want a specific feel,
open the appendix, pick a number, come back.

---

## 1.3 Watch it fall

We are running the physics but we cannot see it. That is the missing
half of a game. Add the paint loop.

```scheme
;; A bouncing ball, frame by frame. A real cabinet paints each position;
;; here we collect the ball's position each frame into a trace so we can
;; see the motion as data (the UPDATE loop, with DRAW replaced by "record").
(define (trace-bouncing-ball frames)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 40) (vec/make 0 0)))
             (n frames)
             (acc '()))
    (if (= n 0)
        (reverse acc)
        (let ((nb (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.85)))
          (loop nb (- n 1) (cons (body-pos nb) acc))))))

(trace-bouncing-ball 5)
;; => ((160 40.5) (160 41.5) (160 43) (160 45) (160 47.5))
```

The trace shows the ball accelerating downward: the gaps between y-values
grow each frame, which is gravity doing its work. Run it for more frames
and you would watch y climb to 240, reverse, and rebound.

Each iteration of the loop does two jobs: `step` advances the physics
(the UPDATE loop) and `cons`-ing the position onto `acc` records it (the
DRAW loop — on a real cabinet this is where you would paint a circle at
`(body-pos nb)`). The named `loop` packages both into the ORCHESTRATION.
On hardware the runtime's `on-frame` callback would call the same two
steps sixty times a second; the shape of the code is identical.

Which brings us to the four patterns.

---

## 1.4 The four patterns, right now

Every game in this book uses four architectural patterns. This chapter
shows them at their smallest.

**Composition.** The ball is not one thing. It is a position plus a
velocity plus a bounce coefficient (and later a shape and a tag). We
compose those into one `body` value — a `(pos vel)` pair — and pass the
bounce coefficient into `step`. Later chapters compose more: mass,
friction, drag, gravity-scale, sensor flag, collision layer, each a new
field in the body value. Composition is how a raw pair-of-vecs becomes a
character.

**Orchestration.** The frame loop is an orchestration of update, draw,
poll — the classic game triangle. Each tick we call `step` (update),
record or paint the position (draw), and later we will read
`input/pressed?` (poll). Order matters: update before draw, or the drawn
frame lags reality. Poll before update, or input gets delayed by one
frame. Chapter 6 formalizes this ordering as FEEL; here you are living it
without naming it.

**In-routing.** In this chapter there is no user input, so no in-route.
Chapter 2 adds it: a key-press routes to a paddle handler. For now, the
only 'incoming event' is the frame tick itself, which routes to one turn
of the loop.

**Routing.** Draw calls route to the framebuffer. On a real cabinet that
routing is implicit — a paint call knows where to send its pixels. When
we add scoring, `cortex/write` will route the score to persistent memory.
Every action-verb the game emits routes somewhere.

Every subsequent chapter will name these four when they appear. That is
not padding. Those four patterns are the whole shape of how any
interactive program works, arcade or otherwise. If a kid finishes this
book, they carry those patterns into anything else they build.

---

## 1.5 Rain

One ball is a lesson. Thirty balls is a scene.

```scheme
;; A rainstorm of 30 balls. The world is just a list of bodies.
(define (make-rain n)
  (map (lambda (i) (body (vec/make (+ 20 (* i 10)) 20) (vec/make 0 0)))
       (iota n)))

;; Step every body one frame with the same rules (map over the list).
(define (step-world world gravity floor-y bounce)
  (map (lambda (b) (step b gravity floor-y bounce)) world))

;; Run the whole world forward `frames` frames.
(define (run-world world gravity floor-y bounce frames)
  (if (= frames 0)
      world
      (run-world (step-world world gravity floor-y bounce)
                 gravity floor-y bounce (- frames 1))))

(define rained (run-world (make-rain 30) (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.7 100))
(list (length rained)                     ;; how many balls
      (cadr (body-pos (list-ref rained 0)))) ;; y of the first, at rest
;; => (30 237.20000000000016)
```

`iota 30` builds the list `(0 1 2 ... 29)`. The `map` in `make-rain`
turns each index into a `body` at a starting position — thirty bodies in
one list. `step-world` is another `map`: it applies `step` to every body,
giving the next world. `run-world` calls `step-world` over and over.
Iterating a whole world is just mapping a per-body function over the list.

Run it and the 30 balls fall from a line at the top, bounce with a
satisfying decay, and settle into a rough heap along the floor (y near
237, just above the floor at 240). There is no game here — no goal, no
score — but there is motion, and motion at scale is the first fun a
computer can give you.

---

## 1.6 Rain you cause

Add input. Now the rain comes from where you click.

```scheme
;; Press 'a: a ball drops from the top. Here `presses` is a script of
;; booleans — one per frame — standing in for live input. On a real
;; cabinet you would replace `(car ps)` with `(input/pressed? 'a)`.
(define (step-world world)
  (map (lambda (b) (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.75)) world))

(define (rain-on-command presses)
  (let loop ((ps presses) (world '()))
    (if (null? ps)
        (length world)                      ;; how many balls exist
        (let ((world2 (if (car ps)          ;; pressed this frame?
                          (cons (body (vec/make 160 20) (vec/make 0 0)) world)
                          world)))
          (loop (cdr ps) (step-world world2))))))

;; Five frames; pressed on frames 1, 3, 5 → three balls.
(rain-on-command (list #t #f #t #f #t))
;; => 3
```

This one is a game. Not because it has a win state, but because the
player CAUSES the physics. Every press adds another ball. The heap grows.
The kid discovers, in about ten seconds, that hammering 'a gives one ball
per press — mash it and you make it rain.

This is the first time the game listens to the player. On a real cabinet
`(input/pressed? 'a)` returns true on the frame the button changed from
up to down, and it takes the place of `(car ps)` in the loop. That is the
in-routing pattern from section 1.4: an incoming event (button press)
routes to a handler (`cons` a new body onto the world). The event source
is the input driver; the handler is our `if` clause; the router is the
runtime, which polled the button before the frame.

If you wanted the ball to spawn where the player points rather than at
x=160, you would read `input/buttons` for a pointer position. For now,
one spawn point is enough for one lesson.

---

## 1.7 Corner physics

Same rainstorm, one extra rule: gravity is not vertical. Tilt it.

```scheme
;; Gravity tilted so a ball drifts toward the bottom-right corner.
;; step-fric adds floor-drag: when a body sits on the floor, we shrink
;; its horizontal velocity by `friction` so it does not slide forever.
(define (step-fric b gravity floor-y bounce friction)
  (let* ((v (vec/add (body-vel b) gravity))
         (p (vec/add (body-pos b) v)))
    (if (> (cadr p) floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car p) floor-y)
              (vec/make (* (- 1 friction) (car v)) (* -1 bounce (cadr v))))
        (body p v))))

(define (corner-rain)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 20) (vec/make 0 0))) (n 100))
    (if (= n 0)
        (body-pos b)                        ;; final position
        (loop (step-fric b (vec/make 0.2 0.5) 240 0.6 0.1) (- n 1)))))

(corner-rain)
;; => (1063.3193999999992 238.736)
```

Gravity of `(vec/make 0.2 0.5)` accelerates slightly to the right AND
down, so the ball drifts far right (x past 1000) while resting on the
floor (y near 240). The `friction` term gives it floor-drag so it does
not slide forever — without it the x would run away even faster.

Try flipping the sign: `(vec/make -0.2 0.5)` drifts left. Try a negative
y-component: `(vec/make 0 -0.3)` and the ball floats up like helium. This
is the gift of physics-as-arithmetic — one number changes and the whole
world changes with it.

---

## 1.8 A small game — pile in the corner

Now put a goal on it.

```scheme
;; Press 'a to spawn a ball. Try to pile 10 balls into the bottom-right
;; corner (x > 260, y > 220). `presses` scripts the input; the result is
;; (count won?).
(define (step-fric b gravity floor-y bounce friction)
  (let* ((v (vec/add (body-vel b) gravity))
         (p (vec/add (body-pos b) v)))
    (if (> (cadr p) floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car p) floor-y)
              (vec/make (* (- 1 friction) (car v)) (* -1 bounce (cadr v))))
        (body p v))))
(define (step-world world)
  (map (lambda (b) (step-fric b (vec/make 0.2 0.5) 240 0.5 0.1)) world))

(define (in-corner? b)
  (and (> (car (body-pos b)) 260) (> (cadr (body-pos b)) 220)))

;; Count matches with fold (SRFI-1): walk the world, add 1 per match.
(define (count-in-corner world)
  (fold (lambda (b acc) (if (in-corner? b) (+ acc 1) acc)) 0 world))

(define (pile-challenge presses)
  (let loop ((ps presses) (world '()))
    (if (null? ps)
        (let ((c (count-in-corner world))) (list c (>= c 10)))
        (let ((w2 (if (car ps)
                      (cons (body (vec/make 160 20) (vec/make 0 0)) world)
                      world)))
          (loop (cdr ps) (step-world w2))))))

;; Spawn 12 balls over 12 frames, then coast 80 frames while they drift.
(pile-challenge (append (map (lambda (i) #t) (iota 12))
                        (map (lambda (i) #f) (iota 80))))
;; => (12 #t)
```

Five ingredients — gravity, floor, spawn on input, iteration over the
world, and a win predicate — combine into a game with a goal. The
goal-check is a routing decision: read the world state, decide if it
matches the win condition, route to a "YOU WIN." Later chapters will
route that same decision to a scoreboard, a sound, a new level.

Note the shape of the win-check. `count-in-corner` uses `fold` (SRFI-1):
it walks every body, threading a running count and adding 1 on each
match. That is exactly the "count matches" idiom — and `fold` is the
honest name for it. No mutable counter needed; the accumulator does the
work.

---

## 1.9 Common desktop tasks

An operator running a shop can drop this ball-physics into a real
customer interaction. Two patterns you will actually see:

**The flip-the-coin unlock.** A shop wants to reward the kid who scrolls
to the bottom of the item listing with a 5% discount. Insert a small
game: a coin (a body stepped with `bounce` = 0.9) drops from the top; the
shopper taps to flip it; if it lands heads (a `random-int` check on the
landing frame), the discount unlocks and the shop's Cortex writes
`(cortex/write 'discount-earned #t)`.

**The bounce-a-ball checkout treat.** After a shopper hits 'buy,' show a
30-second rainstorm with the shop's brand color, one ball per item they
bought. If they bought fifteen items, fifteen balls rain. The rain is
the receipt. Physics as decoration is decoration you cannot fake with
a static image.

Both patterns use only what this chapter covers. No new verbs. The point
of the arcade book is not that you write only arcade games — it is
that arcade primitives ARE the building blocks of any interactive
surface.

---

## 1.10 Pitfalls

**The ball falls through the floor.** Your `step` never checks the floor,
or you set `floor-y` above the ball's spawn y. With no floor test the
body has nothing to hit; it falls forever.

**The ball never stops.** Set `bounce` to something less than 1. At
exactly 1, no energy is lost and the ball bounces forever. Real rubber is
around 0.7 to 0.85; a super-ball is 0.9 to 0.95. Above 1 is non-physical
and worlds get unstable — but if you WANT chaos, pass `bounce` = 1.05 for
one run and watch the ball leave the top of the screen.

**Nothing moves.** You forgot to thread the stepped body back into the
loop — a common named-let slip is calling `(loop b ...)` with the old `b`
instead of `(step b ...)`. The body just sits there. Check that every
recursive call passes the *new* body.

**The rainstorm is slow.** Fifty entities is fine; five thousand may
strain the runtime. Chapter 5 introduces entity pooling for high
counts. For chapter 1, cap yourself at a hundred.

**The `iota` call errors.** `iota` is in the standard Scheme library
and should be available. If your runtime version does not have it,
build it with `(define (iota n) (let loop ((i 0) (acc '())) (if (= i
n) (reverse acc) (loop (+ i 1) (cons i acc)))))` — but you will
almost certainly not need to.

---

## 1.11 Try this yourself

1. Change gravity to `(vec/make 0 0.1)` and watch the rain fall in slow
   motion. Change it to `(vec/make 0 2)` and watch it fall so fast you
   barely see it.
2. Change `bounce` to 0.99 and run the rainstorm for more frames. What
   happens to the heap?
3. Add a mass field to `body` and scale gravity by it inside `step`. Does
   mass change the fall speed? Should it? (Answer: on a frictionless
   world, no — Galileo's tower experiment. Scaling both the force and the
   acceleration by mass cancels. Try it and see.)
4. Lower `floor-y` from 240 to 100 partway through the run. The floor
   moves up under the balls. What happens?
5. Add a constant lateral wind by folding `(vec/make 0.2 0)` into the
   gravity term inside `step`. Now the rain drifts.
6. Spawn balls at random y-positions instead of a straight line — the
   rain becomes a soup. What does the heap look like?

Each experiment takes about a minute and teaches you one number's worth
of physics.

---

## 1.12 What we built

One body with gravity, floor, and bounce. Then thirty of them in a list.
Then input-triggered spawn. Then tilted gravity. Then a win predicate.
Then two shop patterns using nothing but this chapter's primitives.

The next chapter adds a paddle. The paddle catches the ball, and the
ball reflects. Reflection is the first rule of arcade physics beyond
gravity, and it is the seed of every game that has a wall in it —
Pong, Breakout, pinball, Arkanoid, Peggle. Read chapter 2 with your
thumb ready.

---

## 1.13 Reference

Primitives used in this chapter, all documented in the Motoi Scheme
reference:

- vec: `vec/make`, `vec/add`, `vec/scale`
- input (on a real cabinet): `input/pressed?`, `input/buttons`
- persistence: `cortex/write`; randomness: `random-int`

Core forms used: `define`, `let`, `let*`, `let loop` (named let),
`lambda`, `if`, `map`, `fold` (SRFI-1), `iota`, `list`, `list-ref`,
`length`, `car`, `cadr`, `cons`, `append`, `min`, `+`, `-`, `*`, `>`,
`>=`, `=`, `and`, `null?`.

The game loop is written by hand on real primitives: a body is a
`(pos vel)` pair of vecs, the world is a list of bodies, `step` is a pure
function advancing one frame, and a named `let` runs the frames.")

(code-block :chapter 1 :lang "scheme" :source
"(define (body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (body-pos b) (car b))
(define (body-vel b) (cadr b))
(define (step b gravity floor-y bounce)
  (let* ((v (vec/add (body-vel b) gravity))
         (p (vec/add (body-pos b) v)))
    (if (> (cadr p) floor-y)
        (body (vec/make (car p) floor-y)
              (vec/make (car v) (* -1 bounce (cadr v))))
        (body p v))))
(define (bouncing-ball-anchor frames)
  (let loop ((b (body (vec/make 160 40) (vec/make 0 0))) (n frames))
    (if (= n 0)
        (body-pos b)
        (loop (step b (vec/make 0 0.5) 240 0.85) (- n 1)))))
(bouncing-ball-anchor 60)")
